 A returned `outcome: checkpoint` keeps the reservation live and the agent resumable. The cheap move is always the same: read the report and the actual diff, run `dispatch-budget-grant.mjs --agent-id <id> --extra 30 --reason "<why>"`, then `SendMessage` to the SAME agent id with a procedural continuation (make one read-only call first; continue only after the `DISPATCH-BUDGET-GRANT` notice names the digest). Grants are repeatable and each is a recorded decision. A fresh dispatch for the same outcome is the exception, named by a typed reason (`elephant-restart`, `owner-lost`, `context-poisoned`, `new-vertical-outcome`) and by the checkpointed outcome it does not continue. When the estimate already exceeds the base cap, plan the grant in the package (`budgetRationale`) instead of discovering it at the checkpoint. The rule text is `roles/elephant.md` EL-25a and EL-13b.
 
+`dispatch-budget-grant.mjs` is for Claude agents only; do not read it for another runner. On Codex the route is the checkpoint plus a narrow continuation: the agent returns `outcome: checkpoint` with its continuation card (done, remaining, next step), and the Coordinator dispatches one narrow continuation limited to the remaining step, instead of a grant.
+
 The generator reminds the dispatcher at build time and never refuses (D196): `DBG-CHECKPOINTED-OWNER` names another started, unfinished slice of the same item with the grant-and-resume commands and the release route (`slice-queue.mjs cancel <id> --started --reason "<why>" --class budget`) and is not raised for a slice whose recorded write scope is disjoint from this one (D205: a parallel slice); `DBG-ESTIMATE-ABOVE-BASE` fires when `estimatedToolCalls` exceeds the base cap and `budgetRationale` does not say a grant is planned; `DBG-ESTIMATE-NEEDS-SPLIT` asks for a split along independent criteria when the estimate exceeds twice the base cap plus one grant step of 30.
 
